# VIRTUAL-LAB: A Collaborative 2D Physics Sandbox

## Technical Report

---

**Project Title:** Virtual-Lab — Collaborative Physics Sandbox for Education  
**Team:** Siddharth Bohra  
**Repository:** [github.com/Siddharth-ji-Bohra/Virtual-Lab](https://github.com/Siddharth-ji-Bohra/Virtual-Lab)  
**Date:** May 2026

---

## 1. Abstract

Virtual-Lab is a full-stack, real-time collaborative 2D physics sandbox designed for physics education. Built using **React 19**, **Matter.js**, and **Socket.io**, the platform enables users to create rigid-body experiments with circles, rectangles, and constraints (ropes, springs, pivots, motors, welds), simulate them under Newtonian mechanics, and observe live analytics dashboards showing velocity, kinetic energy, and net force graphs. A built-in library of 8 pre-configured physics experiments — from simple pendulums to domino chain reactions — allows instant exploration of key physics concepts. The system supports real-time multi-user collaboration through WebSocket-based room management, enabling shared physics simulations with live cursor tracking.

---

## 2. Introduction

### 2.1 Problem Statement

Traditional physics education relies on static diagrams and mathematical formulas, making it difficult for students to develop an intuitive understanding of concepts like momentum transfer, oscillation, or friction. Existing physics simulation tools (PhET, Algodoo) are either single-user, platform-dependent, or lack real-time collaboration capabilities suitable for modern classroom environments.

### 2.2 Objective

To build a browser-based, real-time collaborative physics sandbox that:

1. Allows users to **create and simulate** 2D rigid-body experiments interactively
2. Provides a **library of 8 pre-built physics experiments** for guided learning
3. Enables **real-time multiplayer collaboration** via WebSocket rooms
4. Displays **live analytics** (velocity, energy, force) as simulations run
5. Supports **property inspection and editing** of individual physics bodies

### 2.3 Scope

The project covers a complete full-stack implementation including a React frontend with a canvas-based physics renderer, a Node.js/Express backend with Socket.io for real-time communication, JWT-based authentication, and Docker deployment configuration.

