## 12. Limitations & Future Work

### 12.1 Current Limitations

1. **No Persistent Login** — Authentication exists in the backend but is not yet wired to the frontend UI
2. **Single-Instance Backend** — Socket.io rooms are stored in-memory (not Redis), limiting horizontal scaling
3. **Canvas Size Dependency** — Experiment templates are designed for ~950×420px; different screen sizes may cause minor offset issues
4. **No Mobile Support** — Touch interactions are not optimized for phone/tablet screens

### 12.2 Future Enhancements

1. **Onboarding Tour** — Guided walkthrough using intro.js for first-time users
2. **Experiment Recording** — Record and replay simulations as video
3. **Custom Experiment Saving** — Allow users to save and share their own experiments to the cloud
4. **3D Mode** — Extend to Three.js / Cannon.js for 3D physics simulations
5. **AI-Assisted Learning** — Integrate an LLM to explain physics concepts as experiments run

---

## 13. Conclusion

Virtual-Lab successfully demonstrates a production-quality, real-time collaborative physics sandbox built entirely with modern web technologies. The system's combination of a robust physics engine (Matter.js), elegant state management (Zustand), real-time collaboration (Socket.io), and a polished UI (React + Tailwind) creates an engaging platform for interactive physics education. The modular architecture allows easy extension with new experiment types, additional analytics, and future 3D capabilities.

